{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. I’m Dr. Lee, one of the hospitalists. You were admitted for a severe headache. Can you tell me more about it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, it started about a week ago. It’s sharp and throbbing in my forehead, and light really bothers me. I’ve had migraines before, but this one won’t go away."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. You went to the ER and had a spinal tap, correct? The opening pressure was normal—15—and no infection or bleeding was found."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, that’s right. They sent me home, but the headache came back. I’ve been taking Tylenol, but it doesn’t help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Understood. Your blood pressure was high in the ER—180 over something—but it’s come down now. We’ll keep an eye on that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m on blood pressure meds already. Amlodipine, lisinopril, and spironolactone. Should I be worried?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll continue those. Your current reading is 117 over 82, which is good. We’re more focused on the headache right now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. I also feel nauseous, but no vomiting. And no vision changes or weakness."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s reassuring. We’ve reviewed your MRI and MRV from this morning. No signs of a stroke, tumor, or blood clots in the brain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:39:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I was so nervous about the MRI. I almost couldn’t do it. They gave me something to help me relax."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, the note mentioned that. But we got good images. The only thing we see is some chronic small vessel disease in the pons, but it’s unchanged from before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:41:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not a new stroke or something serious?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, nothing acute. Given your history of migraines and pseudotumor cerebri, and now a normal spinal tap, this looks like a severe migraine with tension-type features."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:43:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "But my pseudotumor—could that be flaring up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:44:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Unlikely. The spinal tap pressure was normal, so we don’t think it’s increased intracranial pressure this time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:45:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that’s a relief. What’s the plan?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll focus on pain control. You’re allergic to NSAIDs, so we can’t use those. We’ve given you Tylenol and increased your tizanidine. We’ll also try oxycodone for breakthrough pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:47:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve taken oxycodone before. It helps, but I get constipated."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:48:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll give you a stool softener and polyethylene glycol to prevent that. Also, we tried IV caffeine earlier, but it didn’t help much."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:49:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve heard about trigger point injections. Could that help?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2139-02-17 09:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Neurology agrees. We’ve scheduled you for a follow-up in the pain clinic to discuss that option."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2139-02-17 14:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi again. How’s the headache now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2139-02-17 14:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Still there, but a little better since the last dose of oxycodone."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2139-02-17 14:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. Your labs came back. There’s a note about a hemolyzed potassium sample, so we can’t trust that value. But your kidney function shows a mild acute rise in creatinine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2139-02-17 14:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Acute kidney failure? That sounds scary. Is it serious?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2139-02-17 14:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s mild and likely due to dehydration or the pain meds. We’re not giving you any nephrotoxic drugs, and we’re keeping you well-hydrated. It should improve."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2139-02-17 14:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had gastroparesis for years. Could that be making it harder to stay hydrated?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2139-02-17 14:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Possibly. We’re monitoring your intake. You’re on Zofran for nausea, which should help with stomach emptying."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2139-02-18 10:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Morning. You slept through the night?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2139-02-18 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, mostly. The headache is still here, but not as intense."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2139-02-18 10:07:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s progress. We’ll keep adjusting your pain meds. Your diabetes is stable—your glucose has been in the 120s."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2139-02-18 10:08:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m on glyburide. Should I keep taking it twice a day?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2139-02-18 10:09:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, and we’ve adjusted the dose slightly to 1.5 tablets twice daily. We’ll send that home with you."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2139-02-18 10:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my Topamax? I take 50 in the morning and 100 at night."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2139-02-18 10:11:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re reducing it to 50 mg total daily—two 25 mg tablets in the morning and one 100 mg at night—for now, to avoid side effects while you’re on other meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2139-02-19 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news—we’re discharging you today. Your headache is better controlled, and your kidney function is improving."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2139-02-19 08:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. What should I do when I get home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2139-02-19 08:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take the oxycodone as needed, but not more than every four hours. Always with the stool softener and polyethylene glycol. Tylenol every six hours if needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2139-02-19 08:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the pain clinic appointment?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2139-02-19 08:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, you have an appointment with Dr. ___ next week for possible trigger point injections. Keep your follow-up with your PCP too."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2139-02-19 08:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I won’t drive while on the oxycodone, and no alcohol—I know."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2139-02-19 08:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. And call us if the headache worsens or you develop new symptoms like vision changes or weakness."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2139-02-19 08:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I feel much better than when I came in."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2139-02-19 08:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll get your discharge papers ready. You should be out by 10 a.m."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2139-02-19 08:39:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "One more thing—my blood pressure meds. Lisinopril was changed?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2139-02-19 08:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we reduced it to 20 mg daily because your pressure has been stable. The rest stay the same."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2139-02-19 08:41:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And simvastatin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2139-02-19 08:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Now 10 mg twice daily. Lower dose to be cautious with your kidneys."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2139-02-19 08:43:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Thanks for explaining everything."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2139-02-19 08:44:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well. Just take it easy at home and keep up with your follow-ups."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2139-02-17 09:27:00",
    "admission_end": "2139-02-19 15:20:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with a history of pseudotumor cerebri, migraines, type 2 diabetes, hypertension, and gastroparesis was admitted for a severe, persistent frontal headache with photophobia and nausea. She had a normal lumbar puncture with opening pressure of 15 and no signs of infection or elevated intracranial pressure. MRI and MRV showed no acute intracranial process, with only chronic small vessel ischemic changes unchanged from prior imaging. Her headache was managed with acetaminophen, oxycodone, tizanidine, and Zofran, as NSAIDs and ergotamines were contraindicated due to allergies. She developed mild acute kidney injury, likely due to dehydration or medication effects, which improved with supportive care. Her pain was partially controlled, and she was discharged home with a plan for follow-up in the pain clinic for consideration of trigger point injections. All chronic conditions were stabilized, and medications were adjusted appropriately.",
    "problems": [
      "Migraine, unspecified, without mention of intractable migraine without mention of status migrainosus",
      "Acute kidney failure, unspecified",
      "Benign intracranial hypertension",
      "Gastroparesis",
      "Diabetes with neurological manifestations, type II or unspecified type, not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Other and unspecified hyperlipidemia",
      "Depressive disorder, not elsewhere classified",
      "Benign essential hypertension",
      "Abdominal pain, other specified site",
      "Obstructive sleep apnea (adult)(pediatric)",
      "Other chronic pulmonary heart diseases",
      "Tobacco use disorder",
      "Other acquired absence of organ",
      "Acquired absence of both cervix and uterus"
    ]
  }
}
